  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nMain feature of short term interest is broad mid/upper level \ntroughing making steady east progress across the northern Plains and \nsouthern Canada. Height falls just beginning across northern \nMichigan, with a cold front on the leading edge of these height \nfalls...extending from eastern Lake Superior southwest across \ncentral Wisconsin and Iowa. Respectable deep layer moisture surge \nalong and immediately ahead of this front, as evident by tongue of \nprecipitable water values in excess of 1.50 inches into eastern \nWisconsin and central Upper Michigan. Complete detachment of better \nforcing, both to the north and south, has greatly limited \nshower/storm development across the western Great Lakes. A bit of a \nwarm one otherwise, with pre-frontal warm air advection and still \nplenty of sunshine helping drive temperatures up into the 80s across \na good portion of the Northwoods.\n\nTroughing and attendant cold front will continue to make slow \nprogress into and across our area tonight through Wednesday night, \nkicking off a few rounds of showers and storms in the process. Now \nrather excellent agreement that broad troughing will dominate our \nweather right into the early stages of next week, keeping any real \nheat suppressed well to our southwest and bringing periodic chances \nfor additional showers with any passing embedded waves. \n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nShower and storm evolution through early Thursday. Focus shifts to \ntemperature trends and addressing additional chances for wet weather \nfor the remainder of the period.\n\nDetails: \n\nUpstream front will continue to work slowly across the region \ntonight into early Thursday, perhaps even stalling at times as weak \nwaves (convectively agitated?) work northeast along it. Expect \nshower coverage to increase and become a bit more organized tonight \ninto Wednesday as overhead moisture profiles increase and become \nincreasingly coupled with corridor of enhanced right entrance region \nupper jet dynamics. Some embedded storms are expected given breadth \nof forcing and moisture, but overall limited cape should \nsignificantly throttle back on any severe potential. Focus for most \nwidespread showers does spread southeast with time, with the target \narea for better rain amounts by Wednesday afternoon into early \nThursday centering across northeast lower Michigan...and especially \ndown near Saginaw Bay. Much of the area south of the big bridge \nremains under Marginal Risk Wording for excessive rains Wednesday \ninto Wednesday night, but trends do support any high end rain totals \nremaining isolated at best...with much better heavy rain potential \nremaining off to our southwest. Latest trends are for a bit more \nprogression to the front on Thursday, suggesting most of the active \nweather exiting off to our southeast by Thursday afternoon.\n\nLatest deterministic and ensemble guidance support a bit more \naggressive troughing dominating our weather as we head into the \nweekend and early next week. While hard to offer any real specifics, \nany passing waves in west/northwest flow aloft may kick off some \nshowers at times (but currently not seeing any evidence for \nprolonged periods of wet weather). If some more of the aggressive \ntrough digging scenarios are realized, definitely could see a bit \ncooler temperatures than what is currently advertised in our \nforecast. Plenty of time of course for future adjustments to the \nforecast.",
